1 19-13-104. Powers of governor generally; director,
2 office of homeland security.
3
4 (d) The position of the director, office of homeland
5 security is created in the governor's office and shall be
6 appointed by the governor. He shall be responsible to the
7 governor and may be removed by the governor as provided in
8 W.S. 9-1-202. The director shall:
9
10 (vi) Consult the governor and assist the
11 attorney general and division of criminal investigation on
12 the designation of critical infrastructure zones and the
13 investigation and identification of conveyances, leases and
14 leasehold interests as defined by W.S. 19-13-501(a)(i),
15 within five (5) miles of critical infrastructure zones as
16 disclosed by the recordings reported by county clerks in
17 accordance with W.S. 19-13-501 and 19-13-502.
18
19 19-13-501. Definitions.
20
21 (a) As used in this article:
22
4 HB0097

2025 STATE OF WYOMING 25LSO-0161
1 (i) "Conveyance" means as defined by W.S.
2 34-1-102 and includes conveyances of every instrument in
3 writing by which any interest in real property, surface
4 interests, mineral interests and pore space interests is
5 created, alienated, mortgaged, leased or assigned, or by
6 which the title to any real property may be affected in law
7 or in equity. "Conveyance" shall not include wills;
8
9 (iv) "Designated country or person" means:
10
11 (A) A foreign government or foreign
12 nongovernment person determined to be a foreign adversary
13 by the United States secretary of commerce and specified in
14 15 C.F.R. 7.4(a) 15 C.F.R. 791.4(a); or
15
16 (vi) "Real property" means all interests in land
17 and appurtenances, including structures, affixed thereto,
18 and any intangible characteristic that contributes to the
19 fair market value thereof. "Real property" includes surface
20 interests, mineral interests and pore space interests.
21
22 19-13-502. Critical infrastructure zones; reporting
23 of conveyances; investigations; notification; rulemaking.
5 HB0097

2025 STATE OF WYOMING 25LSO-0161
1
2 (c) Within fifteen (15) days after the a conveyance
3 is executed and completed recorded, the county clerk shall
4 report each the conveyance that involves any property
5 located within a critical infrastructure zone or that is
6 located not more than five (5) miles from a critical
7 infrastructure zone to the director and to the division of
8 criminal investigation.
9
10 (d) Upon receiving a report from a county clerk under
11 subsection (c) of this section, the director and the
12 division of criminal investigation shall determine whether
13 a conveyance is within a critical infrastructure zone or
14 located not more than five (5) miles from a critical
15 infrastructure zone and, upon reasonable suspicion,
16 investigate the conveyance to determine if the conveyance
17 involves a designated country or person or if the
18 conveyance poses a threat to national or state security or
19 to critical infrastructure.
20
21 Section 2. W.S. 19-13-502(b) is repealed.
